WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.524 --> 00:00:17.312
The next morning the rain poured down.

2
00:00:18.413 --> 00:00:22.315
When Mary looked out her window, the moor was almost hidden by gray mist.

3
00:00:23.436 --> 00:00:24.977
There would be no going out today.

4
00:00:24.997 --> 00:00:28.699
"What do you do in your cottage when it rains like this?"

5
00:00:29.159 --> 00:00:30.100
she asked Martha.

6
00:00:32.116 --> 00:00:34.898
"Try to stay out of each other's way," said Martha.

7
00:00:34.918 --> 00:00:37.320
"Dickon likes the wet.

8
00:00:37.781 --> 00:00:40.803
He says he sees things he doesn't see on fair days.

9
00:00:42.004 --> 00:00:46.368
He once found a little fox cub half-drowned in a hole after its mother died.

10
00:00:47.589 --> 00:00:50.471
He brought it home, tucked under his shirt to keep warm."

11
00:00:51.712 --> 00:00:53.734
Mary had grown to like Martha's stories.

12
00:00:55.475 --> 00:01:01.180
She especially liked the ones about Martha's mother, who seemed wise and kind, and her brother Dickon.

13
00:01:02.356 --> 00:01:05.417
"He found a crow another time," Martha went on.

14
00:01:06.597 --> 00:01:08.998
"He brought that one home too and tamed it.

15
00:01:10.058 --> 00:01:12.939
The crow's name is Soot because it's so black."

16
00:01:12.959 --> 00:01:18.541
"If I had a crow or a fox cub, I could play with it," said Mary.

17
00:01:18.561 --> 00:01:20.041
"But I have nothing."

18
00:01:21.362 --> 00:01:22.342
Martha looked puzzled.

19
00:01:22.362 --> 00:01:24.662
"Can you knit or sew?"

20
00:01:24.682 --> 00:01:27.563
"No." "Can you read?"

21
00:01:28.043 --> 00:01:28.724
asked Martha.

22
00:01:28.744 --> 00:01:32.869
"Yes." "Then why don't you read something?"

23
00:01:32.889 --> 00:01:35.351
"I don't have any books."

24
00:01:36.592 --> 00:01:37.392
"That's a pity.

25
00:01:37.913 --> 00:01:38.373
If Mrs.

26
00:01:38.453 --> 00:01:43.036
Medlock would let you go into the library, you'd have thousands of books to read."

27
00:01:44.357 --> 00:01:46.278
Mary did not care about the library.

28
00:01:47.519 --> 00:01:50.841
But hearing about it reminded her of the many rooms in the house.

29
00:01:52.603 --> 00:01:53.823
Were there really a hundred?

30
00:01:54.284 --> 00:01:55.605
Were they really all locked?

31
00:01:56.205 --> 00:01:57.526
Maybe she could count them.

32
00:01:58.707 --> 00:02:00.128
That would give her something to do.

33
00:02:01.380 --> 00:02:04.461
After Martha finished her work, Mary waited a few minutes.

34
00:02:06.161 --> 00:02:09.623
Then she opened her door and set off down the long corridor.

35
00:02:10.923 --> 00:02:16.384
It branched into other corridors, which led her up flights of steps that led to more steps.

36
00:02:18.645 --> 00:02:22.586
There were doors and more doors, and many portraits on the walls.

37
00:02:23.807 --> 00:02:28.008
The men and women wore grand costumes made of satin and velvet.

38
00:02:29.308 --> 00:02:32.651
She came upon a portrait of a plain girl rather like herself.

39
00:02:34.052 --> 00:02:35.092
"Where do you live now?"

40
00:02:35.553 --> 00:02:36.673
Mary asked the girl.

41
00:02:36.693 --> 00:02:38.315
"I wish you were here."

42
00:02:39.836 --> 00:02:42.658
It took a while before she thought to try opening a door.

43
00:02:43.879 --> 00:02:46.621
She was almost frightened when she felt the handle turn.

44
00:02:47.841 --> 00:02:52.885
She stepped into what looked like a bedroom, and on the wall was another portrait of the same

45
00:02:52.945 --> 00:02:53.585
little girl.

46
00:02:55.407 --> 00:02:56.768
Mary opened more doors.

47
00:02:57.964 --> 00:03:00.806
She opened so many that she grew tired of opening them.

48
00:03:02.347 --> 00:03:05.009
There could have been a hundred, though she hadn't counted them.

49
00:03:06.170 --> 00:03:11.934
In one room, which looked like a lady's sitting room, she found a cabinet full of ivory elephants.

50
00:03:13.255 --> 00:03:15.317
She played with them until she became bored.

51
00:03:16.657 --> 00:03:19.860
Mary was shutting the cabinet door when she heard a rustling sound.

52
00:03:21.181 --> 00:03:25.464
On the sofa she spotted a family of mice living in a velvet cushion.

53
00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:32.304
"If they weren't so frightened, I would take them back with me," said Mary, who was ready to

54
00:03:32.344 --> 00:03:33.325
return to her room.

55
00:03:34.686 --> 00:03:36.668
She went out into the corridor again.

56
00:03:38.069 --> 00:03:42.092
Two or three times she lost her way by turning down the wrong corridor.

57
00:03:43.453 --> 00:03:48.397
At last she reached her own floor, although she did not know exactly where she was.

58
00:03:48.417 --> 00:03:51.960
"I believe I've taken a wrong turn again."

59
00:03:53.241 --> 00:03:56.964
She was standing at the end of a short corridor with tapestries on the wall.

60
00:03:58.196 --> 00:03:59.517
"I don't know which way to go.

61
00:03:59.537 --> 00:04:01.438
How still everything is!"

62
00:04:02.859 --> 00:04:05.920
Suddenly the stillness was broken by a strange sound.

63
00:04:07.221 --> 00:04:10.763
It was a cry, but not quite like the one she'd heard before.

64
00:04:10.783 --> 00:04:14.565
"That sounds closer than it was last night."

65
00:04:15.726 --> 00:04:17.447
Mary's heart was beating faster.

66
00:04:17.467 --> 00:04:20.028
"And it is someone crying."

67
00:04:21.309 --> 00:04:26.892
She accidentally touched the tapestry near her and then sprang back, feeling quite startled.

68
00:04:28.204 --> 00:04:32.607
The tapestry covered a door, which swung open and revealed another corridor.

69
00:04:34.229 --> 00:04:38.792
Mrs. Medlock was coming down it with a bunch of keys in her hands and a very angry

70
00:04:38.832 --> 00:04:39.653
look on her face.

71
00:04:41.454 --> 00:04:42.735
"What are you doing here?"

72
00:04:43.215 --> 00:04:46.257
Mrs. Medlock took Mary by the arm and pulled her away.

73
00:04:46.277 --> 00:04:50.300
"What did I tell you about wandering through the house?"

74
00:04:50.320 --> 00:04:54.804
"I went the wrong way, and then I heard someone crying."

75
00:04:56.480 --> 00:04:59.783
"You heard nothing of the sort," said the housekeeper.

76
00:04:59.803 --> 00:05:03.326
"You come along back to your own rooms."

77
00:05:04.607 --> 00:05:10.652
And she half-pushed and half-pulled Mary up one corridor and down another until they were standing in Mary's

78
00:05:10.733 --> 00:05:13.955
rooms. "Now," said Mrs.

79
00:05:14.015 --> 00:05:19.800
Medlock. "You stay where you're told to stay, or you'll find yourself locked up.

80
00:05:21.362 --> 00:05:23.584
Mr. Craven had better get you a governess.

81
00:05:24.788 --> 00:05:27.010
You need someone sharp to look after you.

82
00:05:27.671 --> 00:05:28.712
I've got enough to do."

83
00:05:30.033 --> 00:05:33.636
Mrs. Medlock stormed from the room and slammed the door after her.

84
00:05:34.897 --> 00:05:36.939
Mary sat on the rug before the fire.

85
00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:41.242
She did not cry, but she was pale with rage.

86
00:05:41.262 --> 00:05:45.326
"There was someone crying," she said to herself.

87
00:05:45.346 --> 00:05:46.607
"There was."
